--- !u!114 &922743710
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 922743708}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5f7201a12d95ffc409449d95f23cf332, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 0.19607843, g: 0.19607843, b: 0.19607843, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 12800000, guid: 5a3bc40ee75623843bdbfc28be28f628, type: 3}
    m_FontSize: 63
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 10
    m_MaxSize: 63
    m_Alignment: 0
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: '

    The object of the game is to get the highest score within the selected
    amount of time.



    When you start an unranked game, a game board of the selected
    size is randomly generated. When you start a ranked game, if there exists a game
    board that others have played, but you have not, one is selected for you to play.
    Otherwise, a ranked game is randomly generated.



    The game board consists
    of a number of tiles on a grid. On the edge of each tile is an image. Excluding
    special tiles, an image can be one of three colors, one of three shapes, and
    have one of three fillings.



    Your score is the state of your board:

    -
    In the interior the grid, adjacent tiles have facing edges. On a 3x3 board, there
    are 12 such facing edges. For each facing edge, you score 1 point if the adjacent
    images have the same color, 1 point if they have the same shape, and 1 point
    if they have the same filling.

    - On the exterior of the grid, adjacent tiles
    have adjacent edges. On a 3x3 board, there are 8 such adjacent edges, 2 along
    each of the top, right, bottom, and left of the grid. The scoring for adjacent
    exterior edges is the same as it is for interior facing edges: 1 point each for
    matching color, shape, and/or filling.



    [marked up image]



    The maximum
    possible score for a perfect board is 60 points. The odds that any board would
    be able to score 60 points is astronomically low. The maximum score for a board
    with randomly generated images will be lower. For unranked games, you can configure
    that this maximum score appears while you play.



    To play: Excluding special
    tiles, tap a tile to rotate it 90 degrees clockwise, or drag and drop it to swap
    its location with another tile. Your current score updates as you play. It may
    rise and/or fall as your tiles change position.



    The game ends when your
    timer ends. You can also select to submit your score at any time before this.



    For
    unranked games, your score is recorded and you can view the maximum possible
    score for this board. You may replay the same boards, if you want a chance to
    raise your score. Only your highest score for each specific board is recorded.



    For
    ranked games, your score is recorded and your rank appears, comparing how well
    you did on the board vs other players who played the same board. As other players
    play this board in the future, your rank may fall if other players score higher
    than you. You may not replay ranked games. An overall ranking system using a
    Bayesian weighted average ranking system is used, comparing you to all other
    players, as well as to other players in your country.'
